WEBVTT

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:11.107
A unit test is an automated check of a small behavior.

00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:14.696
We will check a label calculator with normal, zero,

00:00:14.696 --> 00:00:16.104
and negative inputs.

00:00:16.104 --> 00:00:18.778
JUnit Jupiter supplies the test tools.

00:00:18.778 --> 00:00:22.437
It is an external dependency, a library added beyond

00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:24.126
Java's standard library.

00:00:24.126 --> 00:00:27.082
The classpath tells Java where to find it.

00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:27.708
LabelTools multiplies the two counts and rejects

00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:28.816
negative inputs.

00:01:28.816 --> 00:01:32.485
The tests below describe what callers should observe.

00:01:32.837 --> 00:01:35.809
The Test annotation marks a method for JUnit to

00:01:35.809 --> 00:01:36.884
discover and run.

00:01:36.884 --> 00:01:39.793
These public, nonstatic methods check separate

00:01:39.793 --> 00:01:40.425
behaviors.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:43.586
An assertion reports failure when behavior differs

00:01:43.586 --> 00:01:44.914
from the expectation.

00:01:44.914 --> 00:01:48.139
In assertEquals, the expected value comes first and

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:49.720
the actual result second.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.945
For the negative input, fail reports failure if the

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:54.336
call returns normally.

00:01:54.336 --> 00:01:57.561
The catch handles only IllegalArgumentException and

00:01:57.561 --> 00:01:58.762
checks its message.

00:01:58.762 --> 00:02:00.912
Each test supplies its own inputs.

00:02:01.225 --> 00:02:04.781
A test runner finds selected tests, executes them, and

00:02:04.781 --> 00:02:05.900
collects results.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.456
LabelToolsTest dot class is a class literal: it refers

00:02:09.456 --> 00:02:10.774
to the class itself.

00:02:10.774 --> 00:02:14.330
The request selects that class, and a summary listener

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:15.515
collects outcomes.

00:02:15.515 --> 00:02:18.610
The supplied launcher runs the request inside a

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.849
session that closes automatically.

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:24.339
The final print statements read the passed and failed

00:02:24.339 --> 00:02:24.800
counts.

00:02:25.116 --> 00:02:27.464
Predict both counts before running.

00:02:27.464 --> 00:02:30.549
Trace each test, including what happens if the

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:32.628
negative call returns normally.

00:02:32.628 --> 00:02:33.366
Pause here.

00:02:38.301 --> 00:02:41.973
The summary reports three successes and zero failures.

00:02:41.973 --> 00:02:45.170
The exception check counts as a successful test

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.366
because the expected exception occurred and its

00:02:48.366 --> 00:02:49.454
message matched.

00:02:49.454 --> 00:02:53.127
Read both counts: this runner can return normally even

00:02:53.127 --> 00:02:54.351
when a test fails.

00:02:54.682 --> 00:02:57.937
A regression test cycle keeps a behavior check,

00:02:57.937 --> 00:03:01.330
confirms it can fail, and reruns it after repair.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.792
Change the first expected value from six to seven.

00:03:04.792 --> 00:03:08.462
Predict both counts, run it, then restore six and run

00:03:08.462 --> 00:03:08.877
again.

00:03:08.877 --> 00:03:12.201
What evidence shows this check detects the wrong

00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:13.032
expectation?
